SUMMARY:Sept 9 - Physical AI Has a Data Problem. It Isn't Collection Works
 hop
DESCRIPTION:Toronto AI\, Machine Learning and Computer Vision Meetup\nThis
  workshop goes from raw recording to curated corpus. We'll cover what MCAP
  is and why it's built that way\, tour real Physical AI datasets across dr
 iving\, aquatic\, and forest robots\, and open an episode with every senso
 r synced—including channels nothing knows how to decode.\n\n**Time\, Dat
 e and Location**\n\nSep 09\, 2026\n9:00 AM - 10:00 AM PST\nOnline. **[Regi
 ster for the Zoom!](https://voxel51.com/events/document-visual-ai-workshop
 -september-9-2026)**\n\nPhysical AI still relies on familiar computer visi
 on tasks—detection\, segmentation\, depth\, tracking. What's changed is 
 the data unit: no longer a single image and label\, but an episode—a doz
 en sensors ticking on independent clocks for minutes\, with no frame bound
 aries.\n\nMost computer vision tooling assumes the old unit and breaks on 
 the new one.\n\nThat's why Physical AI teams end up with buckets of .mcap 
 files nobody can characterize. Recording is cheap\, so logs pile up faster
  than anyone curates them. Ask what's actually in there—which tasks\, wh
 ich conditions\, how many failures and of what kind—and the honest answe
 r is usually a shrug.\nMCAP has been ROS 2's default log format since Iron
 \, and as of [FiftyOne 1.19](https://docs.voxel51.com/index.html) it opens
  natively: cameras\, LiDAR\, GPS\, IMU\, and logs on one shared timeline\,
  alongside your images and video.\n\nWe'll tackle quality\, the harder hal
 f: what smoothness\, sensor-health\, and outlier metrics actually measure\
 , where each falls short\, and how to turn a score into a defensible decis
 ion.\n\nYou'll leave knowing how to load your own recordings\, query a who
 le corpus instead of a single file\, and which quality signals to trust fo
 r which job.
